issue                                       shipped (incl. inversion)  shipped minus inversion  new
naming diagnostic (real implementation)                          pass                     pass  pass
blanking diagnostic (mutant M2)                                  FAIL                     FAIL  FAIL

Reading:
  - The shipped assertion failed the *real* diagnostic only because of its inverted clause.
    Its substantive clause (pointer + /Invalid discriminator value/) detected the blanking
    mutant, so NE-02's unknown-type half WAS exercised by the shipped test. The inversion
    added no detection, and made the test's own comment ('names the unknown type') false.
  - The new assertions are strictly stronger than the shipped one minus the inversion: they
    also reject a diagnostic that names only part of the legal set.
  - Residual, not quantified here: NE-01's markup loop does `if (frame.ok) continue;` after
    `expect(parsed.ok).toBe(false)`, so an accepting implementation skips the remaining
    assertions in that iteration. The toBe(false) still fails, so the test is not vacuous,
    but the follow-on diagnostic assertions are guarded rather than always run.
